# Netlify header rules — https://docs.netlify.com/routing/headers/
# Netlify applies every rule whose path matches a request, and where two
# matching rules set the same header, later, more specific rules win — so
# the broad `/*` block below comes first with the page default plus the
# security headers, and the four asset-specific blocks that follow each
# override just Cache-Control.

# Everything: HTML pages, including pretty-URL requests such as
# /courses/bronze-obedience, which Netlify serves from the matching *.html
# file on disk while keeping the extensionless path in the browser bar.
# Always revalidate so a rebuild goes live immediately, and carry the
# baseline security headers on every path on the site.
/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()
  # No Content-Security-Policy: the home page's YouTube facade (src/assets/js/
  # site.js, data-video-id on the hero in layout-video.hbs) drops in a
  # youtube.com iframe and thumbnail image at runtime, and a CSP worth having
  # needs careful frame-src/img-src/connect-src entries for that without
  # breaking it. Left for a follow-up once that policy is worked out.

# Hashed build output (generate.js: `assets: { hash: true }`, run by the
# Tailwind pipeline step and kiss's own asset copy) — css/site.<hash>.css and
# js/site.<hash>.js. A changed file gets a new filename, so a cached copy
# never needs to be checked again.
/css/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/js/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Images and fonts keep their filenames across an edit (nothing hashes them),
# so cache for a year without `immutable` — a client can still revalidate if
# the file underneath ever changes.
/images/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000

/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000
